{
  "$comment": "The first body of the biped family (spec 139), and the one its skeleton was measured from. Points up at the family's documents rather than holding them: the clips beside it were never its own. Generated by Studio export from job 2e2671c3-933a-417d-8427-97b7c627e0c9; provenance below is what was actually charged, and does not cover the `slash` clip, which was authored rather than bought (specs 139/143). The `swing` state is `oneshot` where scaffold.ts and the mannequin both say `locking`: a locking state refuses every transition until it finishes -- the refusal is stated once at the top of evaluateTransitions and has no exception -- so a body killed mid-swing would finish the swing and fall half a second later. That is the right trade for a monster and the wrong one for the player, which this unit is. Nothing is given away by it, because the wind-up a locking state protects belongs to the server and this machine cannot reach it; during the swing the only transition that matches is `* -> down`. Spec 164 adds `draw` beside `swing` and a second trigger to reach it: the bow and the sword are two different basic attacks and one `attack` trigger could only ever pick one animation for both, so a player with a bow drawn was swinging a sword at things 400 units away. `draw` is `oneshot` for the same reason `swing` is.",
